  THE MECHANISM, measured rather than assumed. The pinned rule is mode: taint
  with NO rule-level pattern, so it can fire only as source-to-sink
  reachability, and the line-72 result does carry a dataflow trace in both
  arms. What that trace shows is a flow that never leaves one method: the
  source is '$blockId', the parameter of def put declared at
  DiskStore.scala:64, and the sink is the interpolated string at line 72 - the
  two trace steps sit on the SAME line, 72. The engine's DEFAULT taint
  analysis is already intraprocedural, so it resolves this flow on its own;
  --taint-intrafile adds intra-file INTER-procedural propagation, which a
  single-method flow has no use for; and no option at this version disables
  taint analysis. Both arms therefore compute the same result and write the
  same bytes.

  A CORRECTION TO THE MECHANISM AS PREVIOUSLY CHARACTERISED, because it
  changes what a reader should conclude. The QA finding that opened this
  defect described the line-72 result as 'matched by a non-taint pattern'.
