  Reading those measurements. `grep -c "^ \+--"` returns 107 at the default
  width and at COLUMNS=80, over a help text of 558 lines, so terminal width
  does not move the figure; but 107 is not the option count. 110 declarations
  sit at 7-space indent, of which 9 are declared with a short alias first (the
  nine lines listed above, at help lines 25, 53, 106, 149, 236, 252, 332, 379
  and 487) and so can never match a pattern anchored on '--'; and 6 of the 107
  matches are wrapped description continuation lines at 11-space indent that
  merely begin with '--' (help lines 162, 168, 224, 385, 435 and 520). The
  engine's true long-option surface is 110. Of those 110, exactly two are
  taint-named: --taint-intrafile ENABLES intra-file inter-procedural taint and
  its own help text says the fallback is 'intraprocedural analysis only'
  rather than no analysis, and --guarded-taint-signatures attaches
  branch-condition guards to taint effects and requires --experimental. A
  case-insensitive search of the whole help text for 'taint' matches nothing
  but those two names and their own descriptions. There is no --no-taint,
  --disable-taint, --taint-off or equivalent at this version, so a truly
  taint-free arm is not constructible and neither pair claims one.

